 ## Overview
-Packed Tensor Representation is a data structure used in volume rendering to store ray samples where rays have varying numbers of valid samples, avoiding the memory inefficiency of fixed-size tensors with masks when large portions of space are empty [S1]. It packs per-sample ray indices into per-ray chunk information (start index and count) for flattened tensors [S2, unofficial]. Unlike fixed-size storage or mask-based approaches, it replaces a sequence of $N$ samples with a compact representation, which is particularly effective when large portions of the ray consist of transparent or empty space [S1]. It is similar to approaches used in Instant-NGP and PyTorch3D but explicitly described as a solution to the memory inefficiency of $(n\_rays, n\_samples, ...)$ with a mask [S1].
+Packed Tensor Representation is a data structure used in volume rendering to store ray samples where rays have varying numbers of valid samples, avoiding the memory inefficiency of fixed-size tensors with masks when large portions of space are empty [S1]. It packs per-sample ray indices into per-ray chunk information (start index and count) for flattened tensors [S2, unofficial]. It stores valid samples in a contiguous data tensor and uses an integer index tensor to track per-ray start positions and counts, which is particularly effective when large portions of the ray consist of transparent or empty space [S1]. It is similar to approaches used in Instant-NGP and PyTorch3D but explicitly described as a solution to the memory inefficiency of $(n\_rays, n\_samples, ...)$ with a mask [S1].
 
